This directory holds the secrets files. Everything here is gitignored:
nothing is ever committed, packaged into a deploy tarball, or passed on a
command line. Secrets reach the server over the encrypted SSH channel via
stdin only.

## One file per host, and only one of them is active

`scripts/deploy.sh` and `scripts/deploy.ps1` both read `secrets/.env.local`,
and the path is hardcoded in each. With more than one host, keep a file per
host next to it and copy the right one into place before deploying:

    cp secrets/.env.local.<host> secrets/.env.local

There is deliberately no `.env.local` sitting here between deployments. A
missing file makes `deploy.sh` stop and run `secrets-init.sh`, which prompts;
a *wrong* file deploys silently and rotates the wrong host's secrets.

## A stale per-host file is worse than no file

Deploying with an out-of-date copy does not fail — it overwrites
`/etc/sentinel/secrets.env` and rotates whatever differs. Measured on
2026-09-05: the production copy kept here was three weeks old and differed on
six keys, `SENTINEL_BEACON_SECRET` among them. Deploying it would have rotated
the beacon secret, and the external witness would have stopped verifying
signatures — the one component whose whole job is to notice that the server
went quiet.

Refresh a host's copy from the host itself, which is the only source of truth
for what that host is actually running:

    ssh <host> "sudo -n cat /etc/sentinel/secrets.env" > secrets/.env.local.<host>

Verify without printing any value, by comparing SHA-256 prefixes of each key on
both sides.

## Creating a first file

    ./scripts/secrets-init.sh

It generates `SENTINEL_DB_PASSWORD`, `SENTINEL_SESSION_SECRET` and
`TELEGRAM_CALLBACK_HMAC_KEY`, and prompts for the rest. It does **not** invent
a Telegram bot token: two instances sharing one token cancel each other out in
`getUpdates`. See `docs/TELEGRAM.md` section 8.
